WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.419
Peers and Cross-Team Communication.

2
00:00:02.699 --> 00:00:06.731
Day 63 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:08.481 --> 00:00:09.505
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.785 --> 00:00:12.729
Most of the time you have influence but no authority.

5
00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:17.212
You can't order the product manager, the QA lead or another team around.

6
00:00:17.492 --> 00:00:22.335
You persuade them by building a relationship and making your ask easy to say yes to.

7
00:00:23.685 --> 00:00:24.538
Part 1.

8
00:00:24.818 --> 00:00:26.461
Know what each role cares about.

9
00:00:27.711 --> 00:00:29.055
Here is a quick table:

10
00:00:29.335 --> 00:00:31.852
Role, and Cares about, and Speak their language.

11
00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:33.327
Product Manager.

12
00:00:33.607 --> 00:00:34.588
Cares about:

13
00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.553
User value, scope, dates.

14
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:38.177
Speak their language:

15
00:00:38.457 --> 00:00:41.145
This lets users do X; trade-off is Y.

16
00:00:41.425 --> 00:00:42.236
Designer.

17
00:00:42.516 --> 00:00:43.497
Cares about:

18
00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.783
User experience, consistency.

19
00:00:46.063 --> 00:00:47.407
Speak their language:

20
00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:49.564
This interaction is expensive.

21
00:00:49.844 --> 00:00:51.295
Could a simpler pattern work?

22
00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:52.279
QA.

23
00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:53.540
Cares about:

24
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.932
Coverage, edge cases, stability.

25
00:00:56.212 --> 00:00:57.556
Speak their language:

26
00:00:57.836 --> 00:00:59.969
Here are the risky areas to test first.

27
00:01:00.249 --> 00:01:01.807
DevOps or SRE.

28
00:01:02.087 --> 00:01:03.068
Cares about:

29
00:01:03.348 --> 00:01:05.332
Reliability, on-call pain.

30
00:01:05.612 --> 00:01:06.956
Speak their language:

31
00:01:07.236 --> 00:01:08.964
Here's the runbook and the alerts.

32
00:01:09.244 --> 00:01:10.332
Other dev team.

33
00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:11.593
Cares about:

34
00:01:11.873 --> 00:01:14.369
Their own roadmap, API stability.

35
00:01:14.649 --> 00:01:15.993
Speak their language:

36
00:01:16.273 --> 00:01:19.345
Here's the contract change, and here's the migration window.

37
00:01:20.695 --> 00:01:21.975
Sales or Support.

38
00:01:22.255 --> 00:01:23.237
Cares about:

39
00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:24.839
Customer promises.

40
00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:26.463
Speak their language:

41
00:01:26.743 --> 00:01:29.837
This ships on the 15th; here's what to tell customers.

42
00:01:31.187 --> 00:01:32.061
Part 2.

43
00:01:32.341 --> 00:01:33.131
Scripts.

44
00:01:34.381 --> 00:01:36.237
Asking another team for something.

45
00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:37.455
You could say:

46
00:01:37.735 --> 00:01:39.741
Hi Neha, I'm on the checkout team.

47
00:01:40.021 --> 00:01:44.266
We need a new reserveStock endpoint by the 20th to launch flash sales.

48
00:01:44.546 --> 00:01:46.978
I've drafted the API contract (link).

49
00:01:47.258 --> 00:01:48.965
Could your team review it this week?

50
00:01:49.245 --> 00:01:53.085
If it helps, we can raise the PR ourselves and you just review.

51
00:01:54.435 --> 00:01:56.504
Negotiating scope with a PM.

52
00:01:56.784 --> 00:01:57.723
You could say:

53
00:01:58.003 --> 00:01:59.283
I love this feature.

54
00:01:59.563 --> 00:02:04.405
In the 2 weeks we have, we can deliver filters and sorting, but not saved searches.

55
00:02:04.685 --> 00:02:06.285
Which matters more for the launch?

56
00:02:06.565 --> 00:02:08.891
We can add saved searches in the next sprint.

57
00:02:10.241 --> 00:02:12.182
Disagreeing in a design review.

58
00:02:12.462 --> 00:02:13.401
You could say:

59
00:02:13.681 --> 00:02:15.878
I see the benefit of event sourcing here.

60
00:02:16.158 --> 00:02:19.507
My concern is the extra complexity for a team of three.

61
00:02:19.787 --> 00:02:25.121
What if we start with a simple outbox table and move to event sourcing if audit needs grow?

62
00:02:25.401 --> 00:02:27.214
Happy to be convinced otherwise.

63
00:02:28.564 --> 00:02:30.441
Following up when they've gone silent.

64
00:02:30.721 --> 00:02:31.660
You could say:

65
00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.311
Hi Arjun, following up on the contract review from Monday.

66
00:02:35.591 --> 00:02:38.023
We need it by Thursday to keep the launch date.

67
00:02:38.303 --> 00:02:41.033
If you're swamped, a 10-minute call might be faster.

68
00:02:41.313 --> 00:02:42.977
Does tomorrow at 11 work?

69
00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:45.522
Thanking publicly.

70
00:02:45.802 --> 00:02:46.741
You could say:

71
00:02:47.021 --> 00:02:49.453
Big thanks to @Kavya from the platform team.

72
00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:53.509
She stayed late to fix the Kafka config so we could release on time.

73
00:02:54.859 --> 00:02:55.755
Part 3.

74
00:02:56.035 --> 00:02:57.912
Building cross-team relationships.

75
00:02:59.162 --> 00:03:00.186
Coffee chats:

76
00:03:00.466 --> 00:03:03.751
20 minutes with one person from another team each month.

77
00:03:04.031 --> 00:03:09.023
Help first: review their PR or answer their question before you need something from them.

78
00:03:09.303 --> 00:03:14.167
Close the loop: tell people what happened with their input (We used your idea, and it worked).

79
00:03:14.447 --> 00:03:19.461
Assume good intent: a slow reply usually means they're busy, not that they don't care.

80
00:03:20.811 --> 00:03:21.728
Part 4.

81
00:03:22.008 --> 00:03:23.395
Practice conversation:

82
00:03:23.675 --> 00:03:25.339
Asking another team for help.

83
00:03:26.589 --> 00:03:28.615
Here is how the conversation goes.

84
00:03:28.895 --> 00:03:31.946
Hi Neha, I'm Ashish from the checkout team.

85
00:03:32.226 --> 00:03:33.549
Do you have a minute?

86
00:03:33.829 --> 00:03:35.023
Sure, what's up?

87
00:03:35.303 --> 00:03:40.125
We need a new stock-reservation endpoint for the flash sale on the 20th.

88
00:03:40.405 --> 00:03:42.666
I've drafted the API contract.

89
00:03:42.946 --> 00:03:44.653
The 20th is tight for us.

90
00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:47.130
We're busy with the warehouse migration.

91
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.080
The conversation continues.

92
00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.725
I understand.

93
00:03:52.005 --> 00:03:55.845
Would it help if we wrote the code and your team only reviewed it?

94
00:03:56.125 --> 00:03:57.512
That would help a lot.

95
00:03:57.792 --> 00:04:00.565
Send me the contract and I'll review it by Thursday.

96
00:04:00.845 --> 00:04:01.741
Perfect.

97
00:04:02.021 --> 00:04:03.195
Thank you, Neha.

98
00:04:04.545 --> 00:04:06.081
That's the end of this lesson.

99
00:04:06.361 --> 00:04:11.011
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

100
00:04:11.291 --> 00:04:17.414
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

